# HW-020 - PSRAM strengthened self-test: PASS
# 2026-07-12, M5StickS3 on COM35 (ESP32-S3-PICO-1-N8R8, MAC 98:88:e0:0e:dc:64).
# Image built from c6c6256. A THIRD physical device, independent of HW-018's board.
#
# This re-runs what HW-018 could not. HW-018 passed against the ORIGINAL self-test,
# whose pattern was buf[i] = (uint8_t)(i ^ 0xA5): truncating to a byte makes it repeat
# every 256 bytes, so a stuck A8 would alias 0x0000 and 0x0100, both cells would hold
# 0xA5, and both would be EXPECTED to hold 0xA5. The test passed with the fault
# present, while its own comment claimed it "catches stuck address lines". Only A0-A7
# were ever covered.
#
# The rewritten self-test writes 32-bit words carrying the full index, verifies, writes
# the complement, verifies again, and then runs an explicit power-of-two address-alias
# sweep (write a marker at every 2^n offset, then check none of them aliased).
#
# RESULT: passes on silicon.
#   ROM  : Found 8MB PSRAM device / Speed: 40MHz / octal, vendor 0x0d (AP),
#          64 Mbit, good-die Pass
#   App  : psram: octal SPIRAM mapped; 65536 B of the external heap verified
#          (pattern, complement, address alias)
#   No *** PSRAM SELF-TEST FAILED *** banner; the device stays alive afterwards.
#
# SCOPE, honestly: 64 KiB of an 8 MiB part (0.78%). It proves the ROM detects the
# octal device, the external heap allocation lands in SPIRAM (esp_ptr_external_ram),
# the data lines carry a position-dependent pattern and its complement, and the
# low address lines do not alias within the tested window. It does not exercise the
# whole part.
#
